    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A chiminea is more than a source of heat. It is a centrepiece for outdoor evenings, with roots in Mexican clay-making traditions. The classic design is a rounded, front-loading firebox topped by a narrow chimney. Air is drawn in through the mouth and smoke rises up the flue, so the fire burns hot and most of the smoke goes up rather than into your guests' faces.</p>
<p>Building one yourself is a rewarding project that mixes sculpture with basic masonry. Traditional fired clay is prone to cracking, especially where winters are cold. This guide uses a sturdier method instead: a shell cast from castable refractory, a concrete made to withstand fire, reinforced with metal mesh and set on a firebrick hearth.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Before you start:</strong> check any rules on garden fires and smoke where you live, and read the data sheet for the refractory you buy. Its mixing, curing and heating instructions take priority over the general guidance below.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="materials">1. Materials</h2>
<p>Getting the right materials matters more than anything else in this build. Ordinary concrete is not designed for fire: trapped moisture can turn to steam and blow chunks off the surface, and it weakens under repeated heating. You will need:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Castable refractory:</strong> a dense, heat-rated castable, sometimes sold as refractory concrete, rated well above the heat of a wood fire. Many are rated to around 1,100°C (2,000°F) or more. Refractory mortar is a different product, made for bedding bricks, and is not suitable for casting a shell.</li>
<li><strong>Firebricks:</strong> enough to make a hearth that the chiminea will sit on and that forms the floor of the firebox.</li>
<li><strong>An inflatable exercise ball:</strong> this is the form for the rounded body. Roughly 50 cm (20 in) across suits a garden chiminea.</li>
<li><strong>A cardboard concrete-forming tube:</strong> about 15 cm (6 in) across, to form the chimney.</li>
<li><strong>Reinforcement:</strong> stainless steel mesh or lath, plus wire ties.</li>
<li><strong>Release agent:</strong> vegetable oil or a form-release spray.</li>
<li><strong>Safety gear:</strong> a dust mask (FFP2 or N95), waterproof gloves and safety glasses. Wet cement is caustic, and dry refractory powder should not be breathed in.</li>
</ul>
<p>You may see recipes that mix perlite or vermiculite into the castable. These lightweight minerals make a mix more insulating but also noticeably weaker, which is why oven builders use them for outer insulation layers rather than the wall that faces the fire. For a chiminea, which should be strong and should radiate heat outwards, use the dense castable as the manufacturer supplies it.</p>

<h2 id="planning">2. Planning</h2>
<p>A chiminea draws well when the firebox is much wider than the chimney. A belly of about 50 cm (20 in) with a chimney of about 15 cm (6 in) is a common, workable proportion. The chimney works by the stack effect: hot air in the flue rises, pulling fresh air in through the mouth.</p>
<p>Sketch your design before you start, then mark it on the ball with a permanent marker. Mark where the chimney joins the top and where the mouth will be. The mouth should be large enough to load logs but no larger than it needs to be: an oversized opening lets more smoke spill out of the front.</p>

<h2 id="foundation">3. Foundation and placement</h2>
<p>A finished cast chiminea is heavy, often well over 50 kg, and its base gets very hot. It must never sit on a timber or composite deck, on grass or on bark mulch. Choose a spot on a level, non-combustible surface, such as paving or a concrete pad, in the open air.</p>
<p>Give it plenty of space. Chiminea makers commonly ask for at least 2 metres of clearance from anything that can burn, including the house, fences, sheds, overhanging branches and parasols, and some advise 3 metres. Never use a chiminea indoors, in a garage, or under a pergola, gazebo or covered patio.</p>
<p>Lay a hearth of firebricks on the paving, slightly larger than the chiminea's footprint. Because the cast shell has an open base, these bricks become the floor of the firebox and keep the heat off the ground beneath. If you plan to raise the chiminea on a stand, use a steel stand rated comfortably above the finished weight.</p>
<!--figure:garden-stone-path|Stone paving is non-combustible, but the fire itself still needs to be well clear of planting, fences and the house.-->

<h2 id="formwork">4. Formwork</h2>
<p>The form holds the wet castable in shape while it sets. Inflate the ball to the size you planned and sit it in a bucket or an old tyre so it cannot roll. The part of the ball inside the bucket stays uncovered, which leaves the open base that will sit on your hearth.</p>
<p>Cut one end of the cardboard tube so it follows the curve of the top of the ball, then tape it firmly in place with strong duct tape to form the chimney. For the mouth, tape a shaped block of foam or a cut plastic container to the front of the ball; it will leave an opening once removed. Coat the ball, tube and mouth form generously with release agent so they come away cleanly later.</p>

<h2 id="reinforcement">5. Reinforcement</h2>
<p>Cast refractory is strong in compression but weak in tension, and the shell expands and contracts every time it heats and cools. Mesh helps hold it together if fine cracks appear.</p>
<p>Wrap the ball and chimney tube in stainless steel mesh, snipping it so it follows the curves, and tie the joins with wire. It does not need to be perfectly smooth, but it should be firm. Hold the mesh roughly 1 cm (½ in) off the form with small spacers or by crimping the wire, so it ends up inside the wall rather than on its inner face. Leave the mouth area open.</p>

<h2 id="mixing">6. Mixing</h2>
<p>Mix the castable with clean water in the proportion given on its data sheet, adding the water gradually. Too much water is the most common mistake: it weakens the cured shell, and a wet mix slumps and slides off the curved sides of the ball.</p>
<p>For this method you want a stiff mix, closer to thick, wet clay than to pourable concrete. A handful squeezed in your glove should hold its shape without water running out. Mix only as much as you can place within the working time stated on the bag; most castables begin to stiffen fairly quickly.</p>

<h2 id="casting">7. Casting</h2>
<p>Start at the bottom edge, just above the bucket, and work upwards. Press the castable firmly through the mesh so there are no air pockets, building a wall of about 4–5 cm (1½–2 in) all over the ball and up the chimney.</p>
<p>Smooth the surface with a damp trowel or gloved hand as you go. Build up a little extra thickness where the chimney meets the body, because that joint takes a lot of stress. Keep the thickness even rather than chasing a perfect finish, since thin spots are where cracks start.</p>

<h2 id="curing">8. Curing</h2>
<p>Curing is a chemical reaction, not just drying, and the castable needs water to complete it. As soon as you finish, cover the whole chiminea with plastic sheeting or a damp cloth to stop the surface drying too fast, and keep it in the shade. Follow your product's curing time; many castables need at least 24 hours kept damp and covered.</p>
<p>After about 48 hours, deflate the ball and ease it out through the mouth or the base, and pull out the cardboard tube and mouth form. Then let the shell air-dry somewhere sheltered for at least a week, and longer in cool or damp weather, before any fire goes near it.</p>

<h2 id="finishing">9. Finishing</h2>
<p>Once it is cured, smooth any sharp edges or trowel marks with a masonry rubbing stone or coarse abrasive, wearing your dust mask. Pay particular attention to the rim of the mouth, where hands and logs go in and out. Check inside for loose bits of mesh, tape or cardboard and clear them out.</p>
<p>Many people like the natural, stone-grey look of the cast shell. If you want colour, choose a paint or masonry finish that is rated for high heat, and apply it only to the outside.</p>
<p>Set the chiminea on its firebrick hearth, checking that it sits level and does not rock.</p>

<h2 id="seasoning">10. Seasoning the first fires</h2>
<p>The first fires are the riskiest moment for a new chiminea. Even after curing, the walls still hold water, and if they heat too fast that water turns to steam that can crack the shell or pop flakes off the surface.</p>
<p>Start with a very small fire of crumpled paper and a few sticks of kindling, let it burn for 20–30 minutes, then let it go out and cool completely. Repeat several times over a few days, making each fire a little bigger. Steam or damp patches on the outside are a sign that water is still coming out, so keep the fires small until they stop. Only then is it ready for a full fire.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I use ordinary bagged concrete instead of refractory?</h3>
<p>No. Ordinary concrete is not designed to be heated repeatedly. Trapped moisture can make it spall, and it weakens with each heating and cooling cycle. Use a castable refractory made for high temperatures.</p>
<h3>Should I add perlite to make it lighter?</h3>
<p>Not to the main shell. Perlite makes a mix more insulating but weaker, so it suits outer insulation layers in ovens rather than a wall that faces the fire. A chiminea is meant to radiate heat, so a dense castable is the better choice.</p>
<h3>How do I stop it cracking in winter?</h3>
<p>The biggest risk is water soaking into the shell and then freezing. Keep the chiminea covered with a waterproof cover when it is not in use, fit a rain cap, and if you can, move it into a shed or garage once it is completely cold.</p>
<h3>What is the best fuel?</h3>
<p>Dry, seasoned hardwood such as oak, ash or beech, in pieces small enough to fit the mouth comfortably. Never burn treated or painted timber, plywood or rubbish, and never use petrol, paraffin or other accelerants to start or revive a fire. Avoid charcoal too: it burns hotter and concentrates heat in the base, which a home-made shell may not tolerate.</p>
<h3>Does the chimney need a cap?</h3>
<p>A cap is strongly recommended. A rain cap stops water collecting in the firebox when it is not in use, and a spark guard reduces the chance of embers landing on dry leaves or nearby surfaces.</p>
<h3>How thick should the walls be?</h3>
<p>About 4–5 cm (1½–2 in). Thinner walls are fragile, while much thicker walls add a great deal of weight and take longer to dry out before the first fire.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A home-made chiminea comes down to a few careful choices: a proper castable refractory, a firebrick hearth in a safe open spot, an even, well-reinforced shell, patient curing and slow first fires. Get those right and it will warm your garden for many evenings. If you are planning the space around it, read our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-patio-deck-combo">how to build a patio and deck combination</a>, keeping the chiminea on the paved side, and our <a href="/blog/patio-deck-combo-small-yards">patio ideas for small yards</a>. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your outdoor space with a designer.</p>
